1. Brands run performance marketing campaigns on Facebook through Collaborative Advertisements.
When a person taps on the ad, they’re taken to the retailer’s website or app.
Brands optimize their campaigns and measure return on ad spend based on the purchases of their products on the retailer’s website or app.
Read my article to know how to use Facebook for business.
For example, Iball wireless speakers want to advertise their products on Facebook, but they don’t sell their products through their own site but sell on Flipkart. Considering Iball speaker has a variety of products, they want to serve wireless speaker advertising for Facebook users and direct them to Flipkart page of their wireless speakers.
As per Facebook, all brands have to do is deploy dynamic advertisements that show the right products to people who have an authentic interest. That way, only customers who are likely to buy the product will be directed to the retailer’s site, application, landing page, or anywhere else on the net where the product can be purchased.
You will be able to see potential clients’ products through its dynamic advertising on brand channels, but then you’ll have to finish purchases through retail channels.
The immediate next step is to create a new advertisement account that will solely be reserved for deploying Collaborative Advertisements. After that, you will receive a catalog segment from your merchant partner, which you’re free to review.
Highlighting and showing relevant products to people who have a higher chance of making a purchase
Optimizing online advertisements to boost online sales
Automatically updating existing ads when product availability or price changes
Why do they matter?
More significantly, they need to do this while maintaining positive relationships with their retailing partners. Brands and retailers do not want to go head-to-head to sell products; they will require a modern way of working together.
To learn how products are featured in Collaborative Advertisements, we must first review product Catalogues and Catalogue segments.
How Dynamic Advertisements and Collaborative Advertisements work together?
Collaborative Advertisements – This product takes dynamic advertisements a step further. It allows brands to leverage signals from retailers along with the shared Catalogue segment to run performance marketing campaigns.
Let’s take an instance of a retailer who owns their own product Catalogue made up of many different types of brands and products, like headphones, video recorders, DVD players, and phones.
The retailer can filter their Catalogue by brand with products, such as headphones, DVD players, or phones to create a smaller Catalogue. This smaller Catalogue, a Catalogue segment, may then be shared with the business manager for the brand.
Zack owns a small electronics business called Wire & Spark. He currently sells headphones through retailers and wants to expand his business.
Collaborative Advertisements best practices Every Collaborative Advertisements campaign is unique, but there are some best practices many successful campaigns share.
Campaign objective and duration- The Collaborative Advertisements product is designed for lower-funnel events and works with the Catalogue sales and conversions objectives.
Active traffic campaigns (Brands that want to measure the impact of the traffic they are sending to retailer websites)
When you choose your creative assets, select mobile-optimized automatic placements using your Catalogue segment or product set. Be sure to include the unique value proposition for each product as well as an immediate call to action.
Carousel- Displays multiple products, where each photo in the carousel is pulled from the product set dynamically.
Use retargeting to reach people who have interacted with your products on the retailer’s website or app. We recommend that you start with a retargeting audience of people who viewed your products but did not add to the cart in the last 14 days.
Time Frame – Avoid narrowing down the time frame for events like “viewed or added to cart but did not purchase” or “added to cart but did not purchase.”
Extra Filters – Avoid using targeting filters such as city, interest, or category for retargeting audiences. These are people who already showed interest, and you should reach as many of them as possible.
How to use broad Targeting with Collabrative Advertisements
Broad Audiences To Retargeting – Broad Audiences help bring more people with high intent to your product pages on the retailer’s website or app. This helps grow your retargeting audience pool.
Extra Filters – Avoid using extra targeting filters such as city, interest, or category. Broad audiences already target a narrow range of people who are more likely to be interested in your products.
Purchase Exclusions – Use exclusions to avoid reaching people who already purchased your products within a certain time frame. The right exclusion time frame is based on your product’s purchase cycle.
Create one new ad account for Collaborative Advertisements within the Business Manager. You need to create a new advertisement account for each retailer.
After the retailer shares a Catalogue segment with you, accept the Catalogue segment along with the terms and conditions.
You can create a product Catalogue segment that you can share through your Business Manager to the selected brand. You can also collaborate with multiple brands.
Avoid using extra targeting filters in order to reach more people who are likely interested in your products. To take advantage of conversion optimization and reporting, use the Catalogue Sales and Conversions objectives.
