Of course, one could withdraw this liquidity at any time, but one of the risks is what is known as Impermanent Loss.Simply put, the value of funds withdrawn from a pool by a liquidity provider may be lower than the value originally provided.
By this time, the WETH-WTF trading pair had accumulated dozens of ETH due to bot purchases, and there was almost no WTF left in the pool, which meant that selling any amount of WTF would be a huge profit.
Eventually the liquidity pool becomes fully tilted.And when the bot is sniping at the liquidity, some users who are too FOMO are still spending a lot of ETH to buy WTF in smaller and smaller quantities.
And as of now, the price of WTF is hovering around $0.20.In my opinion, this is all caused by MEV bots, each trying to drain the nascent liquidity pool as fast as possible, hurting each other in the process.It was a zero-sum game, and in the end the robot mentioned above won.
