The application of blockchain technology in the medical field not only enables patients to truly own their own data, but also ensures that clinicians have a comprehensive understanding of existing conditions and treatment options, which is of great help to the medical industry.Today let’s talk about some specific application cases that blockchain may have in healthcare, including health records, medical reimbursement management, and supply chain monitoring.
When a hospital visits a doctor, it is necessary to repeat the examination. For reimbursement of medical expenses, I have to go back and forth with a large number of documents.
Today let's talk about some specific application cases that blockchain may have in medical care.
A blockchain company enables users to store and manage their health information in real time. Users can create lifelong health records that they can take with them when they go to the doctor.Users of the company can use the projectCryptocurrencyto buy more storage space.Like most healthcare blockchain applications, the company has moved to a private blockchain where all nodes are on a secure network.
If you want to create industry-grade solutions that are well suited for using blockchain technology, you have to ensure privacy.This is the real world business reality.
In terms of economic impact, this application of blockchain is critical in medical reimbursement decisions and billing management. According to Frost & Sullivan:
Chronicled is addressing one of the most prominent use cases in blockchain healthcare.They developed a platform to track medicines, blood and human organs.It also uses portable smart sensors that store temperature readings on the blockchain to ensure secure data transmission.
limitation As mentioned above, we have seen that blockchain has brought many benefits to the medical field, but why has the application of blockchain in the medical field not been widely implemented yet?In fact, there are still some problems to be solved in the application of blockchain in the medical industry:
Taking the United States as an example with relatively complete laws and regulations, if a medical institution needs to adopt blockchain technology, it must comply with the earlier regulations on information and privacy for the Internet industry, and the regulations of each state in the United States are different. Similarly, medical institutions need to customize the blockchain system for these rules and regulations in order to meet the standards and launch related services.
The cost of a decentralized or multi-center blockchain is much larger than that of the original centralized network service. The initial investment must be relatively large, which will inevitably hinder the large-scale adoption of blockchain by medical institutions.
