COTA Australia’s role is to promote, improve and protect the wellbeing of older people in Australia as citizens and consumers.
Provides expertise in culturally inclusive policy and practices for the aged services sector.
Protects and enhances the safety, health, well-being and quality of life of people receiving aged care.
For the past year they have been incorporating holistic care into every part of the day.
Once they started to engage with integrated holistic care, their models were changed so that they could priortise relationships with each older person.
All staff who are working with the older person then ensure they are familiar with the person’s important connections, and build this in to conversations and plans.
There’s a lot happening behind the scenes to support this, including nurturing the inner world of each staff member.
Part of the training was to broaden the team’s understanding of spirituality.
They now understand that in places like Australia, spirituality is very broad and may or may not include religious faith.
products – See Me Know Me
Staying Connected – See Me Know Me
Why is it important to stay connected later in life?
“The World Health Organisation views spirituality as inextricably linked to quality of life”.
Just a few examples:
spiritual support (both religious and nonreligious) is a vital factor in well-being and quality of life at end of life (Nichols 2013),
religious involvement and spirituality are associated with better health outcomes including greater longevity, coping skills, health-related quality of life, and less anxiety, depression and suicide (Mueller, Plevak and Rummans 2001),
spirituality can influence older adults’ experience and perception of life events, leading to a more positive appraisal of these events as meaningful (Cowlishaw, Niele, Teshuva, Browning and Kendig 2013).
Research by experts have found that including spiritual care can relieve depression and anxiety (Oh & Kim 2012).
group spiritual reminiscence offered mutual support between group members, sharing in a trusting environment, and new friendships (MacKinlay 2006),
people had a sense of feeling unique and cared for, and others felt stronger to cope following spiritual care interventions (Narayanasamy & Owens 2000), and
Since experiencing spiritual care in action Malinka has enjoyed a greater quality of life.
The carers take a moment to talk with her whilst they support her with dressing and showering.
She has been visited by a new kind of staff member who is really good at listening, and interested in Malinka’s long life story, including exploring her beliefs and values.
Many people think that accepting aged care means they are on the path into invisibility and dependence but in fact with the right care, you can feel more alive than you did before.
